WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.582
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:09.188
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.488 --> 00:00:16.343
Unit twenty: Public Health Policy. Today you will report evidence using passive reporting (is estimated to).

4
00:00:16.643 --> 00:00:19.202
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.620
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.554
Public health.

7
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.848
Public health means the science and practice of protecting the health of populations.

8
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.669
Public health depends on prevention, not just treatment.

9
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:37.924
Your turn. Say: Public health.

10
00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:41.884
Public health.

11
00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:43.616
Prevention.

12
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:47.998
Prevention means action taken to stop disease before it occurs.

13
00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:50.444
Prevention is cheaper than cure.

14
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.482
Your turn. Say: Prevention.

15
00:00:56.333 --> 00:00:57.346
Prevention.

16
00:00:58.083 --> 00:00:59.084
Policy.

17
00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:04.811
Policy means a course of action adopted by a government or organisation.

18
00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:07.927
The policy reduced smoking rates.

19
00:01:08.517 --> 00:01:11.111
Your turn. Say: Policy.

20
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:14.852
Policy.

21
00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:16.850
Vaccination.

22
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.533
Vaccination means giving a vaccine to protect against a disease.

23
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:24.592
Vaccination eradicated smallpox.

24
00:01:25.183 --> 00:01:28.068
Your turn. Say: Vaccination.

25
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:32.236
Vaccination.

26
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:34.193
Tobacco tax.

27
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:39.040
Tobacco tax means a tax that raises the price of cigarettes.

28
00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:42.845
A tobacco tax discourages young people from starting.

29
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.474
Your turn. Say: Tobacco tax.

30
00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:50.636
Tobacco tax.

31
00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:52.257
Equity.

32
00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.689
Equity means fairness in access to health and services.

33
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.200
Health equity means reaching the poorest first.

34
00:02:00.817 --> 00:02:03.365
Your turn. Say: Equity.

35
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.155
Equity.

36
00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:08.883
Evidence.

37
00:02:09.883 --> 00:02:13.067
Evidence means facts or data that support a conclusion.

38
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:15.996
Evidence guided the choice of vaccine.

39
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:19.340
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

40
00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:23.131
Evidence.

41
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:25.001
Population.

42
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:28.829
Population means all the people living in a place.

43
00:02:29.417 --> 00:02:32.450
The whole population benefits from clean water.

44
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.830
Your turn. Say: Population.

45
00:02:38.667 --> 00:02:39.923
Population.

46
00:02:40.733 --> 00:02:44.535
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:48.764
Visal, which health measure has saved the most lives in history?

48
00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:58.804
Vaccination is estimated to save millions of lives every year, and clean water is believed to have saved even more.

49
00:02:59.917 --> 00:03:01.022
Is that proven?

50
00:03:02.117 --> 00:03:10.911
It is thought to be a reliable estimate. Smoking is known to cause cancer, and tobacco taxes are reported to have reduced consumption.

51
00:03:12.017 --> 00:03:13.668
Why use passive wording?

52
00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:24.111
Because the claim rests on studies, not on one person. Findings are said to be strongest when many populations show the same result.

53
00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:26.964
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:27.817 --> 00:03:31.395
Grammar focus: Passive reporting (is estimated to).

55
00:03:31.695 --> 00:03:32.940
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.880
Vaccination is estimated to save millions of lives.

57
00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:39.876
Smoking is known to cause cancer.

58
00:03:40.783 --> 00:03:43.654
Tobacco taxes are reported to reduce consumption.

59
00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:55.472
Use passive reporting verbs (is estimated / believed / known / said / thought / reported) + to-infinitive for claims based on evidence: Vaccination is estimated to save lives. This avoids naming a source.

60
00:03:56.217 --> 00:04:00.750
Be careful! Don't say: Vaccines estimated to save lives.

61
00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.104
Say: Vaccination is estimated to save lives.

62
00:04:06.404 --> 00:04:08.137
is estimated + to.

63
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:11.458
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.267 --> 00:04:13.570
Evidence for Health.

65
00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:19.294
Public health policy aims to protect populations rather than treat individuals one by one.

66
00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:27.176
Vaccination is estimated to save millions of lives every year, and clean water is believed to have saved still more.

67
00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:33.765
Tobacco taxes are reported to have reduced smoking, particularly among young and low-income people.

68
00:04:34.767 --> 00:04:41.631
Equity matters, because the benefits of prevention are known to reach the poorest last unless services are deliberately extended.

69
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:48.395
Policies are said to succeed when they are based on evidence, supported by communities and enforced fairly.

70
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.432
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.283 --> 00:04:57.699
Vaccination is estimated blank save millions of lives.

72
00:04:57.999 --> 00:05:01.026
A, to. B, that. C, for.

73
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:06.520
The answer is A: to.

74
00:05:07.683 --> 00:05:10.937
Action taken to stop disease before it occurs is blank.

75
00:05:11.237 --> 00:05:14.960
A, prevention. B, equity. C, evidence.

76
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.848
The answer is A: prevention.

77
00:05:21.983 --> 00:05:24.796
Fairness in access to health services is blank.

78
00:05:25.096 --> 00:05:28.981
A, policy. B, equity. C, population.

79
00:05:32.533 --> 00:05:34.568
The answer is B: equity.

80
00:05:35.717 --> 00:05:37.577
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:37.877 --> 00:05:44.967
A, Smoking is known to cause cancer.. B, Smoking knows to cause cancer.. C, Smoking is known cause cancer..

82
00:05:48.533 --> 00:05:51.752
The answer is A: Smoking is known to cause cancer..

83
00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:56.251
According to the reading, policies succeed when they are based on…

84
00:05:56.551 --> 00:05:59.636
A, evidence. B, luck. C, rumour.

85
00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.641
The answer is A: evidence.

86
00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:09.997
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.941
Which public health policy has had the greatest impact?

88
00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.204
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:32.504 --> 00:06:55.641
I believe vaccination has had the greatest impact. It is estimated to prevent millions of deaths every year, and it eradicated smallpox entirely. Clean water and sanitation have also been hugely important. The strength of vaccination is that it protects not only individuals but whole communities through herd immunity.

90
00:06:55.941 --> 00:06:57.732
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:58.617 --> 00:07:03.742
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive reporting (is estimated to).

92
00:07:04.042 --> 00:07:09.039
Today's value is evidence-based judgment. Let good evidence guide what you decide.

93
00:07:09.339 --> 00:07:12.236
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:07:12.536 --> 00:07:16.059
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

